-- title:
--   Definition 3, p. 9 — optimality for a measure flow and mean-field equilibrium (Markov policy)
-- statement:
--   Let $\mu=(\mu_t)_{t\ge0}$ be a measure flow and $J_\mu$ the risk-sensitive cost of the model.
--
--   1. A policy $\sigma$ is **optimal for $\mu$** if $J_\mu(\sigma)\le J_\mu(\sigma')$ for every policy $\sigma'$, i.e.
--   $$J_\mu(\sigma)=\inf_{\sigma'\in\Pi}J_\mu(\sigma'),$$
--   the infimum running over all history-dependent policies $\Pi$.
--   2. A pair $(\pi,\mu)$ consisting of a **Markov** policy $\pi$ and a measure flow $\mu$ is a **mean-field equilibrium** if
--   $$\mu=\Lambda(\pi)\qquad\text{and}\qquad\pi\text{ is optimal for }\mu .$$
--
--   The first condition says that the flow is the flow of state distributions generated by $\pi$ (in particular its value at time $0$ is $\mu_0$); the second says that a single agent facing the flow $\mu$ cannot do better than $\pi$, even with history-dependent policies.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Optimality is written as “$\le$ the cost of every policy” rather than as an equality with an infimum; the two agree because costs are real numbers bounded below. Definition 3 allows any policy $\pi\in\Pi$; here $\pi$ is Markov, for which $\Lambda$ is the explicit recursion of p. 9. Since Markov policies are policies, an equilibrium in this sense is one in the sense of Definition 3. The page writes $\pi\in\Phi(\mu)$; $\Phi$ is a misprint for the optimality map $\Psi$ defined just above it.
-- source:
--   Saldi, Başar & Raginsky, Discrete-time Risk-sensitive Mean-field Games, arXiv:1808.03929v2, p. 9, definition of optimality and of Ψ, Λ, and Definition 3

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RiskSensMFG_Existence_Model

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace RiskSensMFG.Existence

variable {X A : Type*} [MeasurableSpace X] [TopologicalSpace X] [OpensMeasurableSpace X]
  [MeasurableSpace A]

/-- A policy `σ` is optimal for the measure flow `μ` (p. 9): `J_μ(σ) ≤ J_μ(σ')` for every policy
`σ'`, i.e. `J_μ(σ) = inf_{σ' ∈ Π} J_μ(σ')`. -/
def IsOptimal (M : Model X A) (μ : ℕ → PM X) (σ : Policy X A) : Prop :=
  ∀ σ' : Policy X A, J M μ σ ≤ J M μ σ'

/-- Definition 3 (p. 9) with a Markov policy: `(π, μ)` is a mean-field equilibrium if `μ = Λ(π)`
and `π` is optimal for `μ` among all (history-dependent) policies. -/
def IsMFE (M : Model X A) (π : MarkovPolicy X A) (μ : ℕ → PM X) : Prop :=
  μ = flow M π ∧ IsOptimal M μ π.toPolicy

end RiskSensMFG.Existence


